**Idempotency Key — Stripe-style**

Client generates a UUID per logical request:

```http
POST /v1/charges HTTP/1.1
Idempotency-Key: 4f8b1e2a-9c0d-4e5f-a1b2-3c4d5e6f7a8b
Content-Type: application/json

{"amount": 1000, "currency": "usd", "customer": "cus_abc"}
```

Server implementation:

```java
@PostMapping("/charges")
public ResponseEntity<Charge> charge(
        @RequestHeader("Idempotency-Key") String key,
        @RequestBody ChargeRequest req) {

    Optional<IdempotencyRecord> existing = idempotencyRepo.findByKey(key);
    if (existing.isPresent()) {
        IdempotencyRecord rec = existing.get();
        if (rec.getStatus() == COMPLETED) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(rec.getHttpStatus())
                .body(rec.getResponseBody(Charge.class));
        } else if (rec.getStatus() == IN_PROGRESS) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(409)
                .body(Charge.error("Original request in progress"));
        }
        // FAILED — depending on policy, could retry or return same error
    }

    IdempotencyRecord record = new IdempotencyRecord(key, IN_PROGRESS, Instant.now());
    try {
        idempotencyRepo.save(record);
    } catch (DataIntegrityViolationException e) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(409).build();  // concurrent duplicate
    }

    try {
        Charge result = paymentService.charge(req);
        record.setStatus(COMPLETED);
        record.setHttpStatus(200);
        record.setResponseBody(result);
        idempotencyRepo.save(record);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(result);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        record.setStatus(FAILED);
        idempotencyRepo.save(record);
        throw e;
    }
}
```

Key design:
- Store with TTL (e.g., 24 hours); older requests won't retry
- Store status (IN_PROGRESS prevents concurrent duplicate processing)
- Atomic insert (DB unique constraint on key) prevents race
- Response body cached; exact same response on retry

**Dedupe table for message consumers**

```java
@KafkaListener(topics = "payment-events")
public void onEvent(PaymentEvent event) {
    if (processedEventsRepo.existsById(event.getId())) {
        return;  // already processed
    }
    try {
        jdbcTemplate.execute(() -> {
            processEvent(event);
            processedEventsRepo.save(new ProcessedEvent(event.getId(), Instant.now()));
        });
    } catch (DuplicateKeyException e) {
        // race: another consumer saved first; that's OK
    }
}
```

Dedupe table has TTL; old entries cleaned up by periodic job. Size depends on throughput and retention.

**Outbox pattern (for publishing events transactionally)**

Instead of:
```java
@Transactional
public void placeOrder(Order o) {
    orderRepo.save(o);      // committed
    kafkaTemplate.send(...); // separate; may fail after commit
}
```

Use outbox:
```java
@Transactional
public void placeOrder(Order o) {
    orderRepo.save(o);
    OutboxEvent event = new OutboxEvent("OrderPlaced", o.toJson(), Instant.now());
    outboxRepo.save(event);  // in same TX
}

// Separate scheduled publisher
@Scheduled(fixedDelay = 1000)
public void publishOutbox() {
    List<OutboxEvent> unpublished = outboxRepo.findUnpublishedLimit(100);
    for (OutboxEvent e : unpublished) {
        try {
            kafkaTemplate.send("orders", e.getPayload()).get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
            e.markPublished();
            outboxRepo.save(e);
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            // will retry next poll; idempotent on consumer side
        }
    }
}
```

The outbox guarantees: if the DB transaction commits, the event is eventually published. At-least-once, consumer dedupes = effectively once.

**Optimistic locking for updates**

```java
@Entity
public class Inventory {
    @Id Long id;
    Integer quantity;
    @Version Long version;
}

@Transactional
public void decrementInventory(Long id, int amount) {
    Inventory inv = inventoryRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    if (inv.getQuantity() < amount) throw new InsufficientStockException();
    inv.setQuantity(inv.getQuantity() - amount);
    inventoryRepo.save(inv);  // throws OptimisticLockException if version mismatch
}
```

If two threads concurrent decrement, one fails with OptimisticLockException. Caller retries; correct end state.

**Annotation style (Spring integration):**

Resilience4j Spring integration applies annotation proxies in the documented outer-to-inner order. The ordering is baked in — declaring `@Retry` before `@CircuitBreaker` in source does *not* change execution order. The runtime always wraps: TimeLimiter → CircuitBreaker → RateLimiter → Retry → Bulkhead. Note the Spring-integration default slightly differs (Bulkhead innermost); this is the documented behaviour from the `resilience4j-spring-boot3` module.

```java
@Service
public class PaymentClient {

    @TimeLimiter(name = "paymentSvc", fallbackMethod = "fallback")
    @CircuitBreaker(name = "paymentSvc")
    @Retry(name = "paymentSvc")
    @Bulkhead(name = "paymentSvc", type = SEMAPHORE)
    @RateLimiter(name = "paymentSvc")
    public CompletableFuture<PaymentResult> charge(Payment p) {
        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> paymentApi.post(p));
    }

    public CompletableFuture<PaymentResult> fallback(Payment p, Throwable t) {
        log.warn("payment fallback for {}: {}", p.getId(), t.getClass().getSimpleName());
        return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(PaymentResult.queued(p.getId()));
    }
}
```

`@TimeLimiter` requires `CompletableFuture<T>` because it runs the work on an executor and cancels on deadline. Synchronous methods cannot use `@TimeLimiter`; use the HTTP client's readTimeout instead.

**Programmatic style — when you need full control:**

The annotation layer hides order. When you want to **prove** to yourself (or in a code review) that composition is right, the fluent `Decorators` API is explicit:

```java
Supplier<PaymentResult> decorated = Decorators
    .ofSupplier(() -> paymentApi.post(payment))
    .withRateLimiter(rateLimiter)       // innermost
    .withBulkhead(bulkhead)
    .withRetry(retry)
    .withCircuitBreaker(circuitBreaker)
    .withFallback(List.of(CallNotPermittedException.class,
                          BulkheadFullException.class,
                          RequestNotPermitted.class,
                          RuntimeException.class),
                  ex -> PaymentResult.queued(payment.getId()))
    .decorate();

// TimeLimiter wraps the async version:
CompletableFuture<PaymentResult> future = timeLimiter.executeCompletionStage(
    scheduledExecutor,
    () -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(decorated::get, executor)
).toCompletableFuture();
```

Read it bottom-up: the supplier is decorated first by RateLimiter (innermost execution), then Bulkhead, Retry, CircuitBreaker, Fallback; TimeLimiter runs async around the whole chain. **The chaining order (inner first in `.with...` calls) reverses the conceptual outer-to-inner order.**

**Reactive WebClient composition:**

```java
Mono<PaymentResult> reactive = webClient.post()
    .uri("/charges").bodyValue(payment)
    .retrieve().bodyToMono(PaymentResult.class)
    .transformDeferred(RateLimiterOperator.of(rateLimiter))
    .transformDeferred(BulkheadOperator.of(bulkhead))
    .transformDeferred(RetryOperator.of(retry))
    .transformDeferred(CircuitBreakerOperator.of(circuitBreaker))
    .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(2))
    .onErrorResume(ex -> Mono.just(PaymentResult.queued(payment.getId())));
```

Same inner-to-outer stacking via `transformDeferred`; `.timeout()` replaces TimeLimiter at the outermost position.
